:name: BMS boot check
:description: Boots the BMS firmware on an emulated Nucleo-H743ZI and captures its console UART.

# Paths are Renode monitor variables so this script works standalone and from
# scripts/boot-check.sh, which overrides them with absolute paths via -e.
# `?=` assigns only if unset, so the caller always wins.
$platform?=@platforms/boards/bms_ecu.repl
$elf?=@systems/powertrain/firmware/bms/build/zephyr/zephyr.elf
$uartLog?=@/tmp/detect-bms-uart.log

mach create "bms"
machine LoadPlatformDescription $platform
sysbus LoadELF $elf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The CAN controller must be attached to a medium.
#
# Without this, Renode's MCAN model has no FrameSent event to raise, logs
#
#   fdcan1: FrameSent is not initialized. Is the controller connected to medium?
#
# and the transmit never completes. The Zephyr driver then waits forever for a
# completion interrupt that cannot arrive, and the firmware's periodic loop stops
# at its first can_send having already printed its banner. The symptom is a board
# that boots perfectly and then goes silent, which reads like a firmware bug and
# is not one: the firmware is correct and the emulated board simply was not
# plugged into a bus.
#
# One node on the hub is enough to make transmission complete. Phase 1 attaches
# the other nodes to this same hub.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
emulation CreateCANHub "canHub"
connector Connect sysbus.fdcan1 canHub

# A file backend, not showAnalyzer. This has to work headless, where there is no
# window to show, and the file backend writes exactly the bytes the firmware
# emitted: no timestamps, no decoration, nothing host-derived. That is what makes
# a sha256 of this file meaningful.
#
# NOTE: this backend does not truncate an existing file, it renames the old one
# aside as <name>.1. scripts/boot-check.sh therefore wipes the whole run
# directory first, so no stale rotation can ever be picked up by a glob and
# folded into the hash.
sysbus.usart3 CreateFileBackend $uartLog true

# Virtual time, not wall clock. RunFor advances the emulation by exactly five
# emulated seconds and stops, so this produces the same result on a loaded
# machine as on an idle one. Determinism starts here.
#
# Five seconds is chosen so the firmware's one-per-second report lands well away
# from the window boundary. A report emitted exactly at the cut would make the
# line count, and therefore the hash, sensitive to rounding.
emulation RunFor "00:00:05"

quit
